subsection.p:
Intuitively, it seems like the probability of getting the car is \frac 12 if you stay and \frac 12 if you switch.
    But this is wrong! The probability of getting the car if you stay is \frac 13 and the probability of getting the car if you switch is \frac 23.
    The reason is that the host's action of opening a door gives you more information about the location of the car.
    We can model this problem using a tree diagram.

  \begin{tikzpicture}
    \node (car) at (25mm,90mm) {car};
    \node (car) at (50mm,90mm) {host opens};
    \begin{scope}[every node/.style={draw, circle, thick,
        anchor=center, font=\large}]
      \node (start) at (0,50mm) {};
      \foreach \n/\l in {1/3,2/2,3/1} {
        \node (D\n) at (25mm,\n*25mm) {Door \l};
      }
      \node (H0) at (50mm,20mm) {Door 2};
      \node (H1) at (50mm,40mm) {Door 3};
      \node (H2) at (50mm,60mm) {Door 2};
      \node (H3) at (50mm,80mm) {Door 3};
    \end{scope}
      \foreach \n in {1,2,3} {
        \draw (start) to node[above] {$1/3$} (D\n);
      }
    \draw (D1) to node[below] {$1$} (H0);
    \draw (D2) to node[below] {$1$} (H1);
    \draw (D3) to node[below] {$1/2$} (H2);
    \draw (D3) to node[below] {$1/2$} (H3);
    \node (prob) at (70mm,90mm) {probability};
    \node (P1) at (70mm,20mm) {$1/3$};
    \node (P2) at (70mm,40mm) {$1/3$};
    \node (P3) at (70mm,60mm) {$1/6$};
    \node (P4) at (70mm,80mm) {$1/6$};
  \end{tikzpicture}

    The tree diagram shows all possible outcomes of the game.
    The first level of the tree shows the three possibilities for the car.
    The second level shows the host's action of opening a door.
    The probabilities of the host's action depend on the car's location.
    The host will always open a door with a goat behind it.
    The probabilities of the host's action are shown on the edges of the tree.
    There are four possible outcomes of the game, listed by the leaves of the tree on the right.
    If you stay at door No. 1, the probability of getting the car is \frac 13 (the first two leaves),
    while if you switch to the remaining closed door, the probability of getting the car is \frac 23 (the last two leaves).
    This is the correct answer to the Monty Hall problem.

statement.p:
Two events A and B are said to be independent
         if the occurrence of one does not affect the probability of the other. Formally, this means:
          P(A | B) = P(A) or equivalently P(A \cap B) = P(A)P(B).
